  "query_preview": "(Today through Friday)...\nIssued at 306 AM EDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nBroad ridging centered over the Plains will amplify while slowly \ndrifting westward. This allows a trough over the northeastern US to \npush into the Ohio River Valley, bringing an end to the current heat \nwave. The ridge, temporarily shunted west, returns next week with \nrenewed heat and humidity. The secondary round of heat does not \nappear to be as prolonged, with another trough arriving late next \nweek along with a reduction in temperatures. Scattered showers and \nthunderstorms are possible with both troughs, though chances are \ngenerally 40 percent or lower due to limited moisture and forcing.\n\nToday will be another hot one for most of the forecast area \nremaining in a heat advisory with max heat indices near to above \n105. The troughing to the NE is expected to finally creep into the \narea, which will likely keep highs below 90 degrees for our NE \ncounties. With the trough creeping in, there will be better chances \nof showers and storms today and into this evening ahead of a \nbackdoor front that will bring a reprieve from the heat for the \nstart of the new week. \n\nAs of early this morning, the cold front is draped over northern \nIndiana and into Ohio, along which a couple lines of thunderstorms \nhave formed. The question for central Indiana is if these storms \nwill reach the forecast area later this morning or if they will stay \njust outside until later in the day. Also watching radar to see how \nthings play out along gust fronts surging south off of the \nstorms. Atmospheric moisture is modeled to increase today with \nbetter lapse rates. With the front acting as an initiating force, \nour convective chances are a lot better compared to the past \ncouple of days. Still, large-scale mid/upper-level forcing is \ndisplaced to the northeast and thus the convective chances are \ndependent on the front itself.\n\nAs mentioned above, still looking at a couple of existing scenarios.\nFirst, the front initiates convection during the early to mid-\nmorning hours, which then propagates southwestward into the \nsouthwesterly low-level flow ahead of the boundary. This would \ngenerally limit severe potential with the primary hazard being heavy \nrainfall and localized flooding.\n\nSecond, the front enters central Indiana dry allowing daytime \nheating to maximize. Increased instability along the front would \nhelp initiate convection during the afternoon, which would propagate \nsoutheastward in line with the shear vector. Bulk shear is not \nexcessive, but enough to allow for some storm-scale organization \ninto clusters or small bowing segments. Steeper lapse rates and \nresidual dryness may present a downburst or wind threat.\n\nBehind the long awaited cold front, which should arrive late \ntonight, will bring cooler and drier air to the area. This airmass \nwill only last a few days before the ridging and heat returns for \nmidweek. Another frontal system should bring a relative dip in \ntemperatures again late week as well as another chance of showers \nand storms sometime mid to late week.",
